locations that will experience flooding include...\nHesperus.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-10-23T22:00:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"La Plata, CO","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.50d9d55c23b1232fd65ae6fe78b20c1a59055270.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.50d9d55c23b1232fd65ae6fe78b20c1a59055270.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Watch","headline":"Flood Watch issued October 22 at 9:50PM MDT until October 24 at 12:00AM MDT by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues to be\npossible.\n\n* WHERE...Portions of southwest Colorado, including the following\nareas, Animas River Basin, Four Corners/Upper Dolores River, San\nJuan River Basin and Southwest San Juan Mountains and Utah,\nincluding the following areas, Arches/Grand Flat,\nCanyonlands/Natural Bridges, La Sal and Abajo Mountains and\nSoutheast Utah.\n\n* WHEN...Through Friday evening.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, recent burn scars and other low-lying and\nflood-prone locations. Low-water crossings may be flooded. Storm\ndrains and ditches may become clogged with debris.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- Flooding is imminent or ongoing with additional rounds of showers\nand thunderstorms expected through Friday afternoon. Rivers and\ncreeks may still experience high waters even after rainfall has\nended.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-10-24T00:00:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Uncompahgre Plateau/Dallas Divide; Northwestern San Juan Mountains; Southwest San Juan Mountains; Paradox Valley/Lower Dolores River; Four Corners/Upper Dolores River; Animas River Basin; San Juan River Basin; Southeast Utah; Arches/Grand Flat; La Sal and Abajo Mountains; Canyonlands/Natural Bridges","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.e0dbcacb680ad71bb79dff518cb60ff22ebdb688.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.e0dbcacb680ad71bb79dff518cb60ff22ebdb688.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Warning","headline":"Flood Warning issued October 22 at 9:44PM MDT until October 23 at 2:00AM MDT by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues.\n\n* WHERE...Western Archuleta County in southwestern Colorado...\nSouthern Hinsdale County in southwestern Colorado...\nSouthern La Plata County in southwestern Colorado...\n\n* WHEN...Until 200 AM MDT.\n\n* IMPACTS...Flooding of rivers, creeks, streams, and other low-lying\nand flood-prone locations is imminent or occurring. Streams\ncontinue to rise due to excess runoff from earlier rainfall.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- At 939 PM MDT, Doppler radar and automated rain gauges indicated\nheavy rain. Flooding is ongoing or expected to begin shortly in\nthe warned area. Between 1 and 2 inches of rain have fallen.\n- This includes the following streams and drainages...\nLa Plata River, Animas River, Vallecito Creek, Taylor Creek, Los\nPinos River, Florida River, and First Fork Piedra River.\n- Some locations that will experience flooding include...\nDurango, La Plata, Falfa, Hesperus, Bondad, Breen, Mayday, Kline,\nMarvel, Redmesa and Vallecito.\n- Flooding along the Animas River, including areas on the north\nside of Durango, is ongoing or expected to begin shortly. Rivers\nand streams will continue to rise through the night, with peak\nflow expected Friday afternoon.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-10-23T02:00:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Archuleta, CO; Hinsdale, CO; La Plata, CO","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.39ae79931bc07893ddeadf7f4c7b717140679ea0.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.39ae79931bc07893ddeadf7f4c7b717140679ea0.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Warning","headline":"Flood Warning issued October 22 at 5:22PM MDT until October 23 at 12:00AM MDT by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"* WHAT...Small stream flooding caused by excessive rainfall is\noccurring.\n\n* WHERE...Southeastern La Plata County in southwestern Colorado...\n\n* WHEN...Until midnight MDT Tonight.\n\n* IMPACTS...Flooding of rivers, creeks, streams, and other low-lying\nand flood-prone locations is imminent or occurring. Numerous roads\nremain closed due to flooding with road washouts also occurring.\nStreams and dry washes continue to rise due to excess runoff from\nearlier rainfall.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- At 520 PM MDT, local law enforcement reported flooding in the\nwarned area. Flooding is already occurring. Between 1.5 and 2\ninches of rain have fallen over the last 24 hours.\n- This includes but is not limited to the following streams\nanddrainages...\nFlorida River, Spring Creek, Ute Creek, Salt Creek and Dry Creek.\n- Some locations that will experience flooding include...\nBayfield, Ignacio, Oxford, Tiffany and Allison.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-10-23T00:00:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"La Plata, CO","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.509ea0de380d3e74ace6819db79d0064e68417e3.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.509ea0de380d3e74ace6819db79d0064e68417e3.001.1"}],"afd":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-10-23T04:53:00+00:00","snippet":"000 FXUS65 KGJT 230453 AFDGJT Area Forecast Discussion National Weather Service Grand Junction CO 1053 PM MDT Thu Oct 22 2026 .KEY MESSAGES... - Several creeks, streams, and rivers have already reached flood stage due to persistent rainfall, and modeled responses suggest several others will do the same overnight. - Impacts from high water levels and flooding may be seen throughout Friday. - A few ","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=GJT&issuedby=GJT&product=AFD&format=CI"},"hwo":null,"fwf":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-10-23T01:16:00+00:00","snippet":".DISCUSSION... This is an automatically generated forecast with no discussion. The normal Fire Weather Forecast will resume in its entirety in the spring. ...GUSTY ERRATIC WINDS CAN B